 ## Discussion
-For these 178 records, the answer to the overall question is a positive, moderately sized descriptive association: Pearson r was 0.546 and the slope was 1.560. The positive direction also held within every class and after class adjustment. Directional consistency therefore survives the class comparison, while the smaller adjusted slope and partial correlation show that the pooled magnitude is not an adequate description of the within-class relationship.
+For these 178 records, the overall association is positive: Pearson r was 0.546 and the slope was 1.560. The positive direction also held within every class and after class adjustment. Directional consistency therefore survives the class comparison, while the smaller adjusted slope and partial correlation show that the pooled magnitude is not an adequate description of the within-class relationship.
 
-Consistency in direction should not be read as consistency in size. Class 2 had the smallest fitted slope, and class 3 had the broadest slope interval. The common-slope model compresses this variation into a weighted summary. Its attenuation relative to the pooled model reflects the different covariance structure after class centering; it does not quantify a causal contribution of cultivar or prove that other sources of variation have been controlled. Rank and omission checks make a single outlier or a purely linear scaling explanation less compelling as the sole explanation of the positive direction, but they do not identify a mechanism.
